  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Skin Type Quiz Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a skin type quiz?\", \"a\": \"A skin type quiz is a short run of questions about how skin feels bare and behaves through the day, ending in a named type. Dermatology usually classifies skin by five types: dry, oily, normal, combination, or sensitive. The quiz on this page asks 8 questions and sorts answers into the first four.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a skin type quiz?\", \"a\": \"Describe it to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder'>AI survey builder</a> in one prompt: the types you want, the questions to ask, and what each result screen shows. Emergent builds the quiz and the logic behind it, and the app saves every finished quiz with its answers. Name any change in a later prompt, from a question's wording to the products on a result screen.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a skincare quiz ask before it recommends products?\", \"a\": \"Ask how skin feels an hour after washing, where shine shows by midday, how pores look, and how a new cream sits after a week. Add one question on scent if you carry fragrance-free lines, and keep the whole skincare quiz under 10 questions so people finish it at a counter or on a bus.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is a Fitzpatrick skin type quiz the same as the one on this page?\", \"a\": \"No. The Fitzpatrick skin type is a classification first developed in 1975 by Thomas Fitzpatrick, MD, with six phototypes going from light to dark. The quiz here sorts by oil and comfort instead, the way a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/style'>style quiz</a> sorts by taste, and its takers land on a routine rather than a phototype.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many questions should a find your skin type quiz ask?\", \"a\": \"Six to 10, if each question separates at least two types. One bare-face question, a midday shine check, pores, a season change, and a blotting test cover the ground without losing anyone. Emergent sets no response caps, and a short quiz can sit on every page of your site at once.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a Baumann skin type quiz based on?\", \"a\": \"The Baumann system is composed of 4 spectra, starting with dry to oily and sensitive to resistant, which cross into 16 skin types. Build on it only if you can write 16 distinct result screens; a four-type quiz keeps every result backed by products you carry.\"}, {\"q\": \"How is a skin type quiz different from seeing a dermatologist?\", \"a\": \"A skin type quiz reads what a person reports; a dermatologist examines the skin itself. Skin type can also change over the course of a life; treat any result as a snapshot. Keep result copy descriptive rather than clinical: a quiz can place someone the way a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/vocabulary'>vocabulary quiz</a> places a reading level, and it should never diagnose.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I run a skin type quiz for free?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Emergent is free to start, and the free tier covers building and publishing. Paid plans add full code export to a GitHub repository you own, which matters once a developer takes the quiz over.\"}, {\"q\": \"How does a skin types quiz tell combination from normal?\", \"a\": \"By where the shine sits. A T-zone that turns shiny by midday while the cheeks stay comfortable points to combination; no shine and no tightness anywhere points to normal. In a matching quiz each option adds a point to one type; write the shine options so the two types never blur.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should the result screen of a what's my skin type quiz offer next?\", \"a\": \"Give the taker somewhere to go: the routine for that type, a link to each product in it, and one opt-in for a follow-up email. A sample offer or a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder/free-trial'>free trial landing page</a> converts takers who want proof before a full-size purchase. The app stores each result beside its answers, and the follow-up can quote exactly what someone said.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
